<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Bentheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1050 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Bad Bentheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Westphalian county on the Dutch border whose sandstone castle and Reformed faith reflected close links to the Netherlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bentheim-castle">Bentheim Castle</a> appears around 1050 to 1116 on a sandstone ridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vechte">Vechte</a> river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border. In the mid 12th century it passed to Otto, a younger son of the counts of Holland, whose descendants ruled it until 1421, when it was inherited by Everwijn of Götterswick, founder of the later house of Bentheim.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was small and rural, known for Bentheim sandstone, quarried and shipped down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vechte">Vechte</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where it was used in buildings such as the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Count Arnold II introduced the Reformed faith around 1588. The house split into several lines, including Bentheim-Steinfurt and Bentheim-Tecklenburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bentheim was pawned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> from 1753 to 1803. In 1806 the county was mediatised and incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a>, then annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After 1815 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a>, and today it forms the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grafschaft-bentheim">Grafschaft Bentheim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-bentheim.jpg" alt="County of Bentheim" width="320" height="413"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Low_Countries_Locator_Bentheim.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1050 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Bentheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arnold II of Bentheim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bentheim, Bentheim-Steinfurt, Bentheim-Tecklenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1428&amp;focus=county-of-bentheim" aria-label="County of Bentheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4841 372 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Bentheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5161 628L5229 694M5161 628L5219 626M5161 628L5154 659"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5219 626h0M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5161 628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5161 628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bad Bentheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1428&amp;focus=county-of-bentheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
